      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is induction better than gas?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most home cooking, yes. Induction is more than twice as efficient at getting heat into the pan, boils water faster, holds a low simmer more steadily, cleans far more easily and adds no combustion pollutants to your kitchen. Gas remains better for round-bottomed wok cooking, charring over a flame and cooking through power outages, and it is often cheaper to install."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is induction cheaper to run than gas?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually not by much, and sometimes not at all. Using DOE test data and EIA national average prices, induction costs about $36 a year to run and gas about $22 to $28. Cheap electricity closes the gap. The exception is if the stove is your last gas appliance, because dropping the gas connection removes a fixed monthly charge that often outweighs the fuel itself."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you use a wok on an induction cooktop?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A flat-bottomed carbon steel or cast iron wok works well on induction for everyday stir-frying. A traditional round-bottomed wok does not, because it will not sit flat or couple with the element, and the sides of the wok stay cooler than they would in a flame. If authentic high-heat wok cooking is central to how you cook, gas is the better choice."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are gas stoves really bad for your health?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Gas stoves measurably raise indoor nitrogen dioxide and can emit benzene, and both are linked to respiratory harm. How much risk that creates in any single home depends on how much you cook, kitchen size and ventilation, and the large health-impact figures in recent studies are modelled estimates. A ducted range hood used every time you cook reduces exposure substantially."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need new pans for induction?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only if your current pans fail the magnet test. Hold a fridge magnet to the base: if it grips firmly, the pan works. Clad stainless, cast iron, enameled cast iron and carbon steel all work. Aluminium, copper and glass generally do not unless they have a bonded magnetic base. Many households find most of their pans already pass."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I run an induction cooktop during a power outage?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not without a backup power source. A full induction range needs a 240V generator connected through a transfer switch or interlock, and it is one of the heaviest loads in a house. A plug-in 1,800-watt induction burner is far easier, running on 120V from a mid-size inverter generator or a large portable power station with enough continuous output."
          }
        },
