        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            Induction and gas are both genuinely good ways to cook, which is why this argument never ends. The useful question is not which technology is better in the abstract but which one suits your kitchen, your wiring and the way you actually cook. Line the two up category by category and induction takes most of them, but the categories gas wins are not trivial, and one of them, installation cost, can decide the whole thing before you have cooked a single meal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Induction is the stronger cooktop on efficiency, speed, low-heat control, safety, cleaning and indoor air. ENERGY STAR puts its energy transfer at roughly 85 percent against about 32 percent for gas, it brings a big pot to the boil faster than any domestic burner, and because there is no combustion it adds no nitrogen dioxide or benzene to your kitchen. The glass surface wipes clean in seconds, and the pan stops heating the moment you lift it off.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gas is the stronger cooktop for round-bottomed wok cooking, for charring peppers and tortillas directly over a flame, and for cooking through a power outage, since most gas burners can be lit with a match. It is also usually cheaper to buy, and if a gas line already runs to your range, swapping one gas range for another costs very little to install. Switching to induction may mean a new 240V circuit or, in older homes, an electrical panel upgrade.
          </p>

