                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cleaning</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flat glass, wipe clean</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Grates, caps and burner heads</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Induction</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you are building, remodeling or already have a 240V range circuit, buy induction; it is the better tool for most home cooking and you will not miss the flame after the first month. If your panel is full, you rely on a wok, or you lose power often and do not own a generator, gas is a perfectly defensible choice, and the sections below show exactly where the trade-offs land. If you do go induction, our guide to the <a href="/best-induction-cookware/">best induction cookware</a> covers the pans that make the most of it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="efficiency-speed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Efficiency and Boil Speed
          </h2>

          <p>
            The efficiency gap comes down to where the heat is made. A gas burner heats air and the bottom of the pan from outside, and most of that hot air simply flows up and around the pan into the kitchen. Induction uses an alternating magnetic field to induce an electric current inside the base of the pan itself, so the metal becomes the heating element. Very little energy is spent heating anything other than the cookware and the food in it.
          </p>

          <p>
            ENERGY STAR's figures, published with its emerging technology award for residential induction cooking tops, put the energy transfer efficiency of gas at approximately 32 percent, conventional electric resistance at 75 to 80 percent, and induction at approximately 85 percent. Other lab tests report somewhat higher numbers for gas depending on pot size and flame setting, and some manufacturers quote induction closer to 90 percent, so treat these as typical rather than exact. Even at the generous end for gas, induction delivers more than twice the heat per unit of energy.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Energy Transfer Efficiency (ENERGY STAR)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~85%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Induction</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">75-80%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Electric resistance</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~32%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Gas</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2.6x</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Induction vs gas, heat delivered per unit</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            That efficiency shows up most clearly when boiling water. Consumer Reports has said that no cooking technology it tests is faster than induction, and in its testing large pots of water reach a boil minutes sooner on induction than on gas or radiant electric. The exact margin depends on the burner, the element and the pot, so be wary of anyone quoting a single universal number. In practice, the pasta water that used to take the better part of a quarter hour on an average gas burner arrives noticeably sooner.
          </p>

          <p>
            Speed applies to preheating too, and this catches gas cooks out. An empty stainless skillet on a high induction setting can reach smoking temperature in under a minute, far faster than the same pan on a gas burner. The power numbers on an induction dial do not map to the flame sizes you are used to, so medium on induction is a lot more heat in the pan than medium on gas. Plan to cook a setting or two lower for the first few weeks.
          </p>

          <p>
            One honest caveat on efficiency: the gas burner's wasted heat is not entirely wasted in winter, because it warms the kitchen. In summer it works against your air conditioning. And efficiency at the pan is not the same thing as cost or emissions at the meter, since a kilowatt-hour of electricity and a therm of gas are priced very differently and generated very differently depending on where you live. That is why the running-cost section below does the arithmetic rather than assuming efficiency settles it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="control-cooking" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Control, Woks and Charring
          </h2>

          <p>
            The old complaint about electric cooking was lag: turn a radiant ring down and the pan keeps cooking for a minute while the element cools. Induction does not have that problem. Because the heat is generated in the pan, turning the power down stops the energy input almost immediately, and the only remaining heat is whatever the pan itself is storing. Response is as quick as gas, and on a well-made cooktop the steps between settings are finer than most gas valves allow.
          </p>

          <p>
            Low-heat control is where induction quietly pulls ahead. Melting chocolate, holding a beurre blanc or keeping a pot of rice at a bare simmer is easy, and many models will hold a warm setting low enough to leave food for long periods without scorching. One caveat worth knowing: on some cheaper units the lowest settings work by pulsing power on and off rather than reducing it smoothly, which you can hear as a cycling click. Heavy pans, such as cast iron, smooth that out almost completely.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where gas still wins</h3>

          <p>
            Wok cooking is the clearest win for gas. A traditional round-bottomed wok sits in the flame, which wraps up the sides and heats a wide bowl of metal so food can be tossed up the walls and back into the hot center. A flat induction surface heats only the flat contact patch, and a round-bottomed wok will not couple with it at all. Flat-bottomed carbon steel woks work well on induction for everyday stir-fries, but they behave more like a deep skillet than a true wok.
          </p>

          <p>
            Charring is the other. Blistering a pepper, toasting a tortilla or scorching an eggplant directly over an open flame simply cannot be done on induction, because there is no flame. The workarounds are reasonable, such as a cast iron pan run very hot, the oven broiler or a handheld kitchen torch, but they are workarounds. If those flame-kissed techniques are a weekly part of your cooking, factor that in honestly rather than assuming you will adapt.
          </p>

          <p>
            Raw power is closer than people expect. The largest zones on many residential induction ranges are rated somewhere around 3,500 to 4,000 watts or more in boost mode, while residential gas power burners commonly run from about 15,000 to 20,000 BTU per hour or so. Because induction delivers far more of its output into the pan, a big induction zone puts at least as much heat into a flat-bottomed pan as most home gas burners do. The difference is shape, not strength: induction heats the base, gas heats the base and the sides.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="air-safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Indoor Air and Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Burning gas indoors produces combustion by-products, and the research on what that does to indoor air has grown considerably. A 2024 Stanford study published in Science Advances measured nitrogen dioxide in more than 100 homes and estimated that gas and propane stoves raise average NO2 exposure to roughly three-quarters of the World Health Organization's guideline for long-term exposure, with the pollution reaching bedrooms within about an hour of a burner being lit and lingering for hours afterwards.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same research group has reported two other findings worth knowing. A 2023 study in Environmental Science and Technology found gas burners and ovens emitting benzene, a known carcinogen, at levels that could push indoor concentrations above health benchmarks, with the benzene coming from the gas rather than the food. A 2022 study led by Eric Lebel found that around three-quarters or more of a stove's methane emissions happen while it is switched off, from small leaks at fittings and connections.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">READ THE RESEARCH CAREFULLY</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Exposure is measured. Health impacts are estimated.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The NO2 and benzene concentrations in these studies were measured in real homes. The headline health figures, such as the Stanford estimate of around 50,000 cases of childhood asthma a year linked to stove NO2, are population-level models built on exposure-response assumptions, and the gas industry disputes them. The direction is consistent across studies; the precise size of the risk in any one home is not known.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The single biggest factor in those studies was how much gas a household burns, followed by whether there is a range hood vented outdoors and whether anyone switches it on. Small homes and small kitchens fared worst. If you keep gas, a ducted hood that captures the front and back burners, run every time you cook, does most of the work. Cooking itself, especially searing and frying, produces fine particles on any cooktop, so ventilation still matters on induction, and an <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-home/">air purifier for the home</a> helps with the particle side, though a HEPA filter does nothing for gases like NO2.
          </p>

          <p>
            On day-to-day safety, induction is simply easier to live with. There is no open flame to catch a sleeve or a dish towel, no gas to leave running unlit, and the cooktop will not heat at all unless a compatible pan is sitting on the zone. The glass does get hot, but only from contact with the hot pan, so it is far cooler than a radiant electric top, and most models show a residual-heat warning. For households with young children or older relatives, that matters more than boil times.
          </p>

          <p>
            One medical caveat: induction cooktop manuals generally advise people with pacemakers or implanted defibrillators to keep a sensible distance from the active zones and to check with their cardiologist. Modern implants are well shielded and the field drops off quickly with distance, but this is a question for your doctor and the device maker, not for a product review.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buy-install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Buying, Installing and Cookware
          </h2>

          <p>
            At the entry level, gas is cheaper to buy. When we checked, the least expensive well-reviewed 30-inch freestanding induction ranges listed at around $1,400, while basic 30-inch gas ranges from the major brands were on sale for under $800 at large retailers. The gap narrows in the mid-range and largely disappears at the premium end, where both technologies cost several thousand dollars. Separate cooktops follow a similar pattern, and prices move constantly, so compare on the day you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Installation is where the real money is decided. A gas range needs a gas connection and an ordinary 120V outlet for the igniters and controls. If gas already reaches your kitchen, a like-for-like swap is cheap. An induction range needs a dedicated 240V circuit, typically 40 amps for a 30-inch range and often 50 amps for larger models, so check the installation manual for the exact figure. If you are replacing an electric range that already has a suitable circuit, you may need nothing more than the right cord.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Situation</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical work needed</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Rough cost range</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Replacing gas with gas</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Reconnect existing line</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low, often included</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Replacing electric with induction, circuit adequate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Plug in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Little or nothing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gas to induction, panel has capacity</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">New 240V 40-50A circuit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $400 to $1,500</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gas to induction, panel full or small</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Panel or service upgrade</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1,500 to $5,000+</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">No 240V possible</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120V battery-assisted range or cooktop</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Around $5,500 or more at list</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those ranges are wide because the work varies enormously. A short run from a panel directly below the kitchen is a modest job; fishing heavy cable through finished walls and ceilings is not. Before assuming you need a new panel, ask an electrician for a proper load calculation, because plenty of homes with 100-amp service turn out to have room for a range circuit once the numbers are run. Where 240V truly is impractical, a few battery-assisted induction ranges and cooktops now plug into a standard 120V outlet, though they cost several times a conventional range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cookware is the cost people forget. Induction only works with pans that have a ferromagnetic base, and the five-second check is a fridge magnet: if it grips the base firmly, the pan will work. Clad stainless, cast iron, enameled cast iron and carbon steel all pass. Plain aluminum, copper and glass do not. Our guide to <a href="/what-pans-work-on-induction/">what pans work on induction</a> goes through each material, and if you own cast iron, the answer to <a href="/does-cast-iron-work-on-induction/">does cast iron work on induction</a> is yes, with a couple of precautions about scratching the glass.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do need new pans, it does not have to be expensive. A seasoned cast iron skillet costs very little and sears superbly on induction, and a fully clad tri-ply set like the Tramontina in our <a href="/best-induction-cookware/">induction cookware guide</a> covers most kitchens for a fraction of premium prices. Avoid relying on interface discs, the steel plates that let non-magnetic pans sit on induction; they work, but they throw away much of the speed and efficiency you switched for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Running Cost, With the Math
          </h2>

          <p>
            Running cost is where the internet gets most confident and least accurate. Induction is more efficient, but electricity costs far more per unit of energy than natural gas in most of the country, and those two facts roughly cancel out. The honest answer depends on your local rates, so here is the arithmetic with every assumption stated, so you can swap in the numbers from your own bills.
          </p>

          <p>
            For annual energy use, we used the Department of Energy's test data from its cooking products rulemaking. In DOE's test sample, smooth-top electric cooktops used roughly 177 to 226 kWh a year and gas cooktops roughly 1,187 to 1,756 thousand BTU a year, based on a representative 418 cooking cycles a year. We took 200 kWh for induction and 1,450 thousand BTU, about 14.5 therms, for gas. The electric sample includes radiant tops as well as induction, so induction may sit nearer the bottom of that range.
          </p>

          <p>
            For prices, we used EIA national averages: residential electricity at about 18.2 cents per kWh for 2026, and residential natural gas at roughly $16 to $20 per thousand cubic feet in recent months, which works out to about $1.55 to $1.95 per therm. Gas prices swing with the seasons and both vary enormously by state, so these are reference points rather than anyone's actual bill.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Scenario</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Induction per year</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Gas per year</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Difference</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">National average rates</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">200 kWh x $0.182 = about $36</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">14.5 therms x $1.55-$1.95 = about $22-$28</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gas cheaper by about $8-$14</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">High electricity rates, 30 cents per kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">200 kWh x $0.30 = $60</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $22-$28</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gas cheaper by about $32-$38</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Low electricity rates, 12 cents per kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">200 kWh x $0.12 = $24</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $22-$28</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly even</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Stove is your last gas appliance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $36</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $22-$28 plus the fixed monthly gas charge</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Induction cheaper by about $45 to $290</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The striking thing is how small every one of those numbers is. Cooking is a minor part of a household energy bill, and the difference between the two fuels is tens of dollars a year at most, which will never pay back a panel upgrade. The one case that changes the picture is a home where the stove is the last gas appliance. Removing the gas connection entirely eliminates the fixed monthly customer charge, which commonly runs somewhere around $5 to $25 depending on the utility. That charge alone can be worth more each year than the gas the stove burns.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rebates and incentives can also shift the purchase math, but programs change frequently and vary by state and utility, so we have not quoted any figures here. Check your utility's website and your state energy office before buying, and get the terms in writing, because some programs require a specific installer or pre-approval before the work is done.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-pick" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Outages, Cleaning and Who Picks Which
          </h2>

          <p>
            Power outages are a real point for gas, with a caveat. On most gas cooktops and ranges you can light the surface burners with a match or long lighter when the power is out, though some models with electronic valves or controls cannot be used at all, so check your manual now rather than during a storm. Gas ovens are a different story: most rely on an electric glow-bar igniter and electronic controls, so they will not run without power. Induction does nothing at all without electricity.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you choose induction and lose power often, plan for it. A full induction range on a 40-amp circuit can draw up to 9,600 watts, and running even two zones at once needs a generator with 240V output connected through a transfer switch or interlock, which is covered in our guide to <a href="/what-size-generator-do-i-need-for-my-house/">what size generator you need for your house</a>. The simpler backup is a plug-in 1,800-watt single-zone induction burner, which runs on 120V from a mid-size inverter generator or a large <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station</a>. If outages are frequent where you live, our picks for the <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">best home generator for power outages</a> are the place to start.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cleaning is not close. An induction top is a flat sheet of glass, and because the glass around the pan stays relatively cool, spills rarely bake on. A wipe with a damp cloth and an occasional pass with cooktop cleaner keeps it looking new. Gas means lifting heavy grates, scrubbing burner caps and clearing ports that clog with boil-overs. The trade is that glass can scratch or, rarely, crack under a heavy impact, and replacement glass on a range is an expensive repair, so lift cast iron rather than dragging it.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are a few smaller quirks on each side. Induction can hum or buzz with some pans, particularly at high power, and the cooling fan may keep running for a minute after you switch off. Gas burners are louder than people notice and add heat and moisture to the kitchen. Neither is a deal-breaker, but if you are sensitive to noise, try an induction burner in a showroom with your own pan before committing.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE DECISION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Pick induction if your wiring allows it. Pick gas if your cooking or your house demands it.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Choose induction if you are remodeling, already have a 240V range circuit, have young children, care about indoor air, or want the fastest and easiest cooktop to clean. Choose gas if your panel cannot take a range circuit without major work, you cook with a round-bottomed wok or char over flame regularly, or you lose power often with no backup plan.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you are on the fence, a portable induction burner is the cheapest experiment in this whole article. Cook on one for a month alongside your gas range, using pans that pass the magnet test. Most people who try it end up preferring induction for everyday cooking, and the ones who do not usually know exactly why, which is the most useful answer of all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Range Before Checking the Panel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The most expensive surprise in a gas-to-induction switch is discovering the panel cannot take a 240V range circuit after the range has been delivered. Get an electrician to run a load calculation and quote the circuit first. It takes an hour and turns an unknown cost into a number you can compare.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Your Pans Will Work</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Plenty of good cookware is aluminum, copper or glass, and none of it will heat on induction. Run a fridge magnet over the base of every pan you own before buying the cooktop, and budget for replacements. Interface discs are a stopgap, not a solution, because they waste much of induction's speed and efficiency.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cooking on Induction With Gas Settings</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Medium on induction is considerably more heat in the pan than medium on a gas burner, and an empty pan can reach smoking point in under a minute. New induction owners burn garlic, scorch oil and warp thin pans by habit. Start lower than you think and preheat with attention for the first few weeks.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Thinking the Switch Fixes Indoor Air on Its Own</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Removing combustion removes NO2 and benzene from the burner, but searing, frying and roasting still produce fine particles and grease on any cooktop. Keep using a range hood that vents outdoors, run it every time you cook, and treat an air purifier as a supplement to ventilation rather than a replacement.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing on Running Cost Alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                At typical rates the annual difference between gas and induction energy costs is tens of dollars, and in either direction depending on local prices. That will never repay a panel upgrade, and it should not tip the decision. Choose on installation cost, cooking style, indoor air and outage resilience, which all matter far more.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is induction better than gas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most home cooking, yes. Induction is more than twice as efficient at getting heat into the pan, boils water faster, holds a low simmer more steadily, cleans far more easily and adds no combustion pollutants to your kitchen. Gas remains better for round-bottomed wok cooking, charring over a flame and cooking through power outages, and it is often cheaper to install.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is induction cheaper to run than gas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not by much, and sometimes not at all. Using DOE test data and EIA national average prices, induction costs about $36 a year to run and gas about $22 to $28. Cheap electricity closes the gap. The exception is if the stove is your last gas appliance, because dropping the gas connection removes a fixed monthly charge that often outweighs the fuel itself.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use a wok on an induction cooktop?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A flat-bottomed carbon steel or cast iron wok works well on induction for everyday stir-frying. A traditional round-bottomed wok does not, because it will not sit flat or couple with the element, and the sides of the wok stay cooler than they would in a flame. If authentic high-heat wok cooking is central to how you cook, gas is the better choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are gas stoves really bad for your health?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Gas stoves measurably raise indoor nitrogen dioxide and can emit benzene, and both are linked to respiratory harm. How much risk that creates in any single home depends on how much you cook, kitchen size and ventilation, and the large health-impact figures in recent studies are modelled estimates. A ducted range hood used every time you cook reduces exposure substantially.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need new pans for induction?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if your current pans fail the magnet test. Hold a fridge magnet to the base: if it grips firmly, the pan works. Clad stainless, cast iron, enameled cast iron and carbon steel all work. Aluminium, copper and glass generally do not unless they have a bonded magnetic base. Many households find most of their pans already pass.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run an induction cooktop during a power outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not without a backup power source. A full induction range needs a 240V generator connected through a transfer switch or interlock, and it is one of the heaviest loads in a house. A plug-in 1,800-watt induction burner is far easier, running on 120V from a mid-size inverter generator or a large portable power station with enough continuous output.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is induction cooking safe with a pacemaker?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most induction cooktop manuals advise people with pacemakers or implanted defibrillators to keep a sensible distance from active cooking zones and to consult their doctor. Modern implants are well shielded and the magnetic field weakens quickly with distance, but the right answer depends on your specific device, so ask your cardiologist or the device manufacturer before switching.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

